\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Fall in Job Openings Signals Cooling Labor Market\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ben Casselman \par \uc2 531 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Employers appear to be responding to rising interest rates and a slowing economy by reducing open positions.\par \par \uc2 The number of job openings fell for the third consecutive month in June, a sign that the red-hot U.S. labor market may be starting to cool off.\par \par \uc2 Employers posted 10.7 million vacant positions on the last day of June, the Labor Department said Tuesday. That is high by historical standards but a sharp drop from the 11.3 million openings in May and the record 11.9 million in March. It was the largest one-month decline in the two decades that the government has kept track of this data, other than the two months at the beginning of the coronavirus pandemic in 2020.\par \par \uc2 The drop was concentrated in retail, the latest sign that the sector is struggling as consumers shift their spending from goods back to services as the pandemic ebbs. But job postings have also fallen in leisure and hospitality, the sector that was the most strained by labor shortages last year.\par \par \uc2 The job market remains strong by most measures. There were still nearly twice as many job openings as unemployed workers in June, and employers are raising pay and offering other incentives to attract and retain staff. Layoffs remained near a record low in June, suggesting that employers were reluctant to part with staff they worked so hard to hire. And the number of workers voluntarily quitting their jobs remains high, although it has fallen from last year's peak.\par \par \uc2 The recent decline in openings is likely to be encouraging news for policymakers at the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , who have been trying to slow down the economy in an effort to tame inflation. \uc2 Jerome H. Powell\uc2 , the Fed chair, and other officials have pointed to the number of vacant jobs as evidence that the labor market is too hot. They are hoping that employers will start posting fewer jobs and hiring fewer workers before they begin laying people off, allowing the job market to cool down without causing a spike in unemployment.\par \par \uc2 Still, any slowdown in the job market will mean that workers have less leverage to demand raises when pay is already failing to keep up with inflation. Slower wage growth, in turn, could lead consumers to spend less, increasing the risk that the United States could slip into a recession.\par \par \uc2 The labor market ''is definitely losing momentum, and that's what is chipping away at people's ability to spend,'' said \uc2 Tim Quinlan\uc2 , a senior economist for \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Economists and policymakers will get a more up-to-date picture of the job market on Friday, when the Labor Department releases data on hiring and unemployment in July. Forecasters surveyed by \uc2 FactSet\uc2  expect the report to show that employers added about 250,000 jobs last month, down from 372,000 in June.\par \par \uc2 The fall in job openings is likely to be encouraging news for officials at the Federal Reserve, who have been trying to slow the economy in the face of high inflation. (PHOTOGRAPH BY NATALIE BEHRING FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220803ei8300044\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
