\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 HarperCollins Strikers Seek Diversity and Higher Wages\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth A. Harris and Alexandra Alter \par \uc2 519 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The one-day action, unionized employees said, was intended to draw attention to core problems with the publishing industry.\par \par \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  union members went on a one-day strike on Wednesday, with around 100 employees and additional supporters marching in front of the company's corporate headquarters in Manhattan in the sticky heat for higher wages, better family leave benefits and a stronger commitment to diversity from the company.\par \par \uc2 Publishing has long offered meager wages to entry and midlevel employees, making it difficult to live in New York City, where the industry is based, without a second job or financial support from a spouse or family.\par \par \uc2 Many workers say that the low wages also make it hard for potential employees who don't come from wealth to consider a career in publishing, which hampers efforts to diversify the mostly white industry.\par \par \uc2 ''I love my job, I love my authors, it's an incredible privilege to get to work on these books, and I would love to do it for the rest of my life, if I can afford to,'' said Stephanie Guerdan, an associate editor in the children's department who joined the strike.\par \par \uc2 But with a salary of $56,000 a year, she said, she worries she won't be able to stay.\par \par \uc2 ''There's a running joke in publishing that you have to have a rich partner to be able to make it in the industry,'' she said. ''That should be a thing of the past.''\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  declined to comment on contract negotiations, which have been going on since December 2021.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the union said that its members, who were mostly women, earn an average of $55,000 per year, with a starting salary of $45,000. Many employees, it added, feel pressure to work long hours without any additional compensation.\par \par \uc2 The union, part of the U.A.W., represents more than 250 employees who work in the editorial, publicity, sales, design, marketing and legal departments. Employees at \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  have been unionized for more than 80 years, the union said, calling it ''one of the earliest unions of 'white collar' workers in the country.'' The union is an outlier in the industry; most big corporate publishers do not have unionized workers.\par \par \uc2 Other major publishing companies have similar starting salaries to those at \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  -- in 2020, Macmillan announced it was raising entry level pay to $42,000, while Hachette raised starting pay to $45,000 in expensive locations.\par \par \uc2 In addition to higher pay, \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  workers are asking for better family leave benefits, a greater commitment to increasing diversity and more union protection.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  has been reporting record breaking profits,'' said Olga Brudastova, the president of Local 2110 of the U.A.W. ''Compensation is not keeping up with the rate of inflation and doesn't reflect the contributions our members make to the company.''\par \par \uc2 Around 100 unionized workers marched outside the HarperCollins headquarters in Manhattan on Wednesday. (PHOTOGRAPH BY BENJAMIN NORMAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220721ei7l0004l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
