\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Weaknesses in the Social Safety Net\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lauren Hirsch \par \uc2 812 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 8\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ''Does worker organizing not work because there's all this monopoly power?'' said Julie Su, deputy secretary of labor. ''Why don't we question whether the monopoly power is the problem.''\par \par \uc2 The gulf between employees and employers feels as vast as ever to many segments of society these days. The coronavirus pandemic laid bare weaknesses in the U.S. social safety net, while severely disrupting the U.S. work force. Workers are rethinking the kinds of jobs they want -- and the expectations they have for them. And employers are desperately seeking a new equilibrium.\par \par \uc2 Is there something structural -- or cyclical -- about this face-off? And what is the role of the government in addressing imbalances? These were among the questions that animated a group of business and policy leaders last week at the DealBook D.C. policy forum.\par \par \uc2 Julie Su, deputy secretary of labor, made the case that government has an implicit role in laying the groundwork for rethinking fundamental beliefs that have long been accepted: for example, that only some people will have jobs with decent wages and will be treated with dignity.\par \par \uc2 ''I think it's a good time to challenge a lot of these notions,'' she said. Addressing the struggle these days over unionizing, she asked: ''Does worker organizing not work because there's all this monopoly power? Why don't we question whether the monopoly power is the problem,'' she said, in an apparent reference to large companies that can fight off labor organizing.\par \par \uc2 The role of monopolies on worker power was just one issue in a vibrant debate that extended over 90 minutes about how the government should address everything from training for jobs to creating new jobs and ensuring that workers are protected in those jobs.\par \par \uc2 It became a heated point of debate when the topic of the minimum wage came up.\par \par \uc2 Some cautioned against government overreach, arguing that policy decisions around the minimum wage, for example, risk affecting employment negatively by increasing the cost for companies without accommodating needs in different industries or areas of the country.\par \par \uc2 ''There's no question that does cost jobs,'' said Harry Holzer, a professor at McCourt School of Public Policy at \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2 . The fix, he suggested, is ''through weekly subsidies,'' though he urged against a federally set minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 Unions could be a way to prevent more overreach from the federal government, said Thomas Kochan, author of ''Shaping the Future of Work: A Handbook for Action and a New Social Contract'' and a professor at M.I.T.\par \par \uc2 ''That's the value of the collective process,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 ''Because collective groups of employees and employers will work out what is viable in different sectors, in different firms, in different occupations -- and that's why rebalancing the power and giving everyone that voice without fear of retaliation is so important. Otherwise, we will have to have more government standards, because that's the only other alternative.''\par \par \uc2 Making it easier to start new businesses across the United States ''is going to be critical to create a more inclusive economy and create the jobs that are going to offset, at least in part, the jobs that will be lost because of disruption and technologies,'' said \uc2 Steve Case\uc2 , the founder of \uc2 AOL\uc2  and chairman and chief executive of Revolution.\par \par \uc2 But creating a more equitable labor dynamic will take more than training. It requires making sure there are enough jobs workers want, the participants agreed. And part of that is making sure that enough investment funds are funneling into businesses throughout the country, not just Silicon Valley, New York and California, where three-fourths of venture funds currently flow, said Mr. Case, citing studies indicating that new companies help drive job creation in the United States.\par \par \uc2 ''Educators need to be preparing students for jobs that provide economic pathways for them that they will have. And employers need to be willing to hire people with skills,'' said \uc2 Ellyn Shook\uc2  of \uc2 Accenture\uc2 . She offered as an example the New York C.E.O. Jobs Council, a coalition of chief executives in New York. ''This is happening and it's working.''\par \par \uc2 Participants: Julie Su, U.S. deputy secretary of labor; Teuila Hanson, senior vice president and chief people officer, \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 ; Thomas Kochan, Author of ''Shaping the Future of Work: A Handbook for Action and a New Social Contract'' and professor emeritus, M.I.T.; Ai-jen Poo, president, National Domestic Workers Alliance; \uc2 Steve Case\uc2 , founder of \uc2 AOL\uc2 , chairman and chief executive, Revolution; Harry Holzer, professor, McCourt School of Public Policy, \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2 ; Zach Dunn, co-founder and vice president customer experience, Robin; Renee Christoff, head of global associate engagement and corporate responsibility, \uc2 T. Rowe Price\uc2 ; \uc2 Ellyn Shook\uc2 , chief leadership and human resources officer, \uc2 Accenture\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY VALERIE PLESCH FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220615ei6f0005h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
