\par\par \uc2 READER COMMENTS\par \uc2 Metropolitan Desk; SECTMB\par \b \uc2 Back to Office? Explain That to the Dog.\b0\par\par\uc2 833 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Back to Office? Explain That to the Dog.\par \par \uc2 Readers responded at nytimes.com to John Leland's article on May 22 about how pet owners are dreading the return to the office. Comments have been edited.\par \par \uc2 Spending the last two and a half years working from home with my dog at my feet under my desk has been great for both me and her. My dog spent half her life with me and my wife out of the house. We did have a dog walker come twice a day and play with her in the backyard, and he would even bring other dogs to hang out. My mom would also swing by and hang out with our dog. But she was still alone the majority of the day. As a lawyer, I've never had more clients and been more productive than I have been working from home. My firm is spread across nine different offices, and the team members I manage are in every office but mine. A return to work for me would see me in my depressing office, door closed, and on Zoom meetings or phone calls all day. My line in the sand is being forced back to work. Luckily (for both me and my dog), my practice is incredibly niche and extremely in demand that I can write my own ticket.\par \par \uc2 John Marshall, New York\par \par \uc2 So many animals have been ''surrendered'' to shelters with return to work, it's an absolute tragedy, and not enough is said or done to address it. While no one wants their pet feeling lonely during the day while at an office, this situation is still a thousand times better than the alternative. Coming home to a pet, feeding them, walking, playing, petting, snuggling, is all so much better than the tragedy of being abandoned to a shelter. This is the reality that is happening. Please, keep your pet and just do your best. The images of an abandoned pet are heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 Oxford Shoes & Commas\par \par \uc2 I was working remotely from September 2020 until January 2021. During this time, our family dog was slowly hitting the wall after being a part of our lives for 13 years. The isolation I experienced being away from my working social environment was a challenge, but I will forever be grateful for being able to spend more time at home giving comfort to our girl before she died in late November 2020.\par \par \uc2 Jon, Wisconsin\par \par \uc2 My dog will be fine. I'm never going in again because of my own mental health. Wasting 90 minutes of my life in traffic to sit in an open-plan office all day is for the birds.\par \par \uc2 Steve, Oakland, Calif.\par \par \uc2 I try to understand why people pay thousands of dollars for dogs. I've always been partial to mutts: less health problems and generally pleasant dispositions. Our pooches have rescued us as much as we rescued them. I get why people got dogs in the pandemic without thinking about returning to work. Hopefully humans and animals can make the adjustment.\par \par \uc2 Sal S, Philadelphia\par \par \uc2 I don't think it's just one's canine or feline pal who doesn't want their caretaker to return to the office. Most people I know and have talked to really don't want to return to the structure and office environment. So many have become as spoiled as their four-legged friends at being home. And man, can I empathize with that feeling. Good luck with the transition everyone.\par \par \uc2 Marge Keller, Midwest\par \par \uc2 I have taken my dogs to work for the last 15 years or so. It's amazing how many co-workers drop into my office, some even twice a day, for a quick dog fix. A couple minutes of holding a dog on your lap and snuggling makes the world a saner place.\par \par \uc2 Ellen, Oregon\par \par \uc2 After our dear old dog died, we waited three months and then went to the county shelter where we spotted a black and white (looked like a Border collie but wasn't) dog sitting in a cage at the back of a mess of howling dogs in cages. She was scared. Had to be given up by her owner for reasons related to him. She had separation anxiety. Believe me, we tried everything since we are both retired and could hire ''experts.'' Nothing worked. Honestly, I think separation anxiety is innate. So now one of us is with her all the time or we take her to day/night boarding when we are out. It's like having a 2-year-old who never grows up. We wouldn't trade her for anything.\par \par \uc2 Mari, Florida\par \par \uc2 It's not that I hate people. I just feel better when I'm with the dogs.\par \par \uc2 Patrick Henry, USA\par \par \uc2 Nala, a goldendoodle, revels in public radio and her owners' socks when they are away. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ADRIENNE GRUNWALD FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220605ei650005m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
