\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Hiring Is Strong Even as the Fed Hits the Brakes\b0\par\par\uc2 By Talmon Joseph Smith, Lydia DePillis and Jeanna Smialek \par \uc2 1,493 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Labor Department reported 390,000 new jobs in May, as policymakers try to ease inflation without inducing a recession.\par \par \uc2 American employers extended an impressive run of hiring in May, even as policymakers took steps to cool the economy in an effort to ease high inflation.\par \par \uc2 The Labor Department reported Friday that employers added 390,000 jobs, the 17th straight monthly gain. The unemployment rate was 3.6 percent for the third straight month, a touch away from a half-century low.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the labor force grew by 330,000 people, and the share of adults employed or looking for work continued to edge closer to prepandemic levels.\par \par \uc2 The data signaled that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's initial moves to dial back its monetary support for the economy were -- at least so far -- not constraining business activity so much that hiring was feeling a pinch.\par \par \uc2 After the strong rebound from the depths of the coronavirus lockdowns -- all but 800,000 of the 22 million jobs that were lost have been recovered -- the Fed has shifted its emphasis from maximum employment to its other mandate: price stability. The challenge is to apply its primary tool, a steady series of interest-rate increases, without inflicting a recession.\par \par \uc2 ''I think we're on sort of what looks like a glide path right now, and that's good -- nothing's broken,'' said Guy Berger, the principal economist at the career-focused social network \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 . ''But keep fast-forwarding it a year and the question marks are still big.''\par \par \uc2 The closely watched indicators include the impact on wages, which have been increasing at a pace not seen in decades, though not enough to keep up with inflation over the past year. The Fed is worried that rising labor costs will be passed along to consumers.\par \par \uc2 On that score, the Labor Department report showed little change in trajectory. Average hourly earnings rose 0.3 percent from the previous month, the same pace as in April, and were 5.2 percent higher than a year earlier, compared with a 5.5 percent year-over-year increase in April.\par \par \uc2 ''It's moderating, but it's not moderating to a level, I think, where it's consistent with the Fed's inflation goals,'' said \uc2 Michael Feroli\uc2 , chief U.S. economist at \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2 , said of wage growth. He said the Fed would probably want wages to cool toward an annualized 3.5 percent pace, at the higher end, a rate that officials view as aligned with 2 percent inflation.\par \par \uc2 President Biden gave a nuanced celebration of the jobs data in remarks on Friday, emphasizing recent gains while arguing that a slowdown would be welcome, allowing inflation to ease.\par \par \uc2 ''The point is this: We've laid an economic foundation that's historically strong,'' Mr. Biden said. ''Now we're moving forward to a new moment, where we can build on that foundation, build a future of stable, steady growth so that we can bring down inflation without sacrificing all of the historic gains that we have made.''\par \par \uc2 Stocks declined on Friday and bond yields rose as investors evidently read the report as reinforcing the Fed's muscular efforts, which risk denting economic growth. ''The better the data, the more difficult that a pause or reduced pace of tightening later this year becomes,'' analysts at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  wrote in a research report published after the jobs numbers were released.\par \par \uc2 The continued job gains are among many indications of a vibrant economy. Reports from the nation's largest banks show checking accounts are still above 2019 levels for nearly all income groups. New bankruptcies and debt-collection proceedings are both at their lowest levels since tracking began in 1999.\par \par \uc2 Yet those encouraging trends have been at odds with the generally sour national mood, dominated by inflation concerns. U.S. consumer sentiment declined in early May to the lowest since 2011, according to the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And with a potential change in the economic cycle, economic security is not evenly distributed among households. If tightening financial conditions prompt businesses to downsize, research shows that ''last hired'' workers will typically be the ''first fired'' -- and that Black and Hispanic workers and those with less education are particularly vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 But a broad range of economists and policymakers, including \uc2 Jerome H. Powell\uc2 , the Fed chair, stress that more modest wage gains when paired with milder prices will be more sustainable for all workers, who are also consumers, in the long run.\par \par \uc2 ''The Fed at this point is saying, look, we'd rather head off inflationary pressures, because if we have to slam on the brakes, then we're going to cause a recession and it will be worse for these underserved communities,'' said Gerald Cohen, an economist at the Kenan-Flagler Business School at the University of North Carolina. ''It's a challenge, because they want to pull people into the labor force. They know the way to do that is through higher wages. But higher wages can also breed higher inflation.''\par \par \uc2 For employers, too, the prospective change in the economic picture may not be spread evenly.\par \par \uc2 ''Businesses with high profitability, easy access to capital, the capacity to automate and pricing power are still eager to hire,'' said \uc2 Bill Adams\uc2 , chief economist at \uc2 Comerica Bank\uc2 , a large commercial bank based in Texas. ''But businesses that are seeing their margins squeezed by rising costs, like hospitality, or that are seeing demand soften, like retail, are pulling job postings as their outlook softens. And competition for workers is squeezing lower-paying employers out of the job market.''\par \par \uc2 Richard Canny, the president of Ultimation in Roseville, Mich., is feeling that squeeze. His company's conveyor belts and simple robots for use in warehousing and distribution sold briskly during the tight labor market, as e-commerce companies sought to make each worker more productive.\par \par \uc2 With 45 employees, Mr. Canny said, Ultimation has managed to stay at full staffing by raising wages -- to $17 to $18 an hour to start, from around $13 to $15 -- as well as offering perks like parental leave and partial tuition reimbursement. His white-collar employees have retained the flexibility to work from home. All that, he said, is necessary to compete with big employers in the area like \uc2 Amazon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Small companies historically haven't had to offer the same kind of benefits as the large ones, but I think that's changed since Covid,'' Mr. Canny said.\par \par \uc2 At this point in the recovery, labor- and business-oriented economists alike believe that expanding the supply of labor may be even more important than jobs gains themselves.\par \par \uc2 There are nearly two openings for every job seeker, and several factors are unlikely to change in the near term. Some, like the large cohort of retiring baby boomers, are simply demographic.\par \par \uc2 More opportunities for women could be part of the answer. Employment of women was up 181,000 in May, yet more than 700,000 below the level in February 2020.\par \par \uc2 That lag may stem, in part, from the lapse of last year's child tax credit expansion, argued Elizabeth Ananat, a professor of women and economics at Barnard College, who has researched the program's effects on parental employment and labor force participation.\par \par \uc2 The credit gave most families monthly payments of up to $250 per older child and $300 per young child, ''helping families weather both inflation and the not-fully-recovered job market, leading to our biggest-ever cuts in poverty for families,'' Ms. Ananat said. For families with a child under 5, she said, a quarter of the credit was being spent on child care.\par \par \uc2 Summer Seay, 34, of Chattanooga, Tenn., worked as a senior customer service representative at \uc2 Cigna\uc2 , the health insurer, throughout the pandemic, making $23 an hour. But she left in late April to spend more time caring for her three children while assisting her husband with ''more of the paperwork'' for the family's wooden pallet business.\par \par \uc2 One reason she decided to leave the wage-earning work force was frustration that much of her pay was merely covering child care costs, which had mounted to $700 a week just as the expanded child tax credit expired.\par \par \uc2 ''It kind of went hand in hand,'' she said. ''Because our big thing is food, feeding the kids.''\par \par \uc2 Another missing element of the work force are recent immigrants. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  researchers said recently that because of visa restrictions and health protocols during 2020 and 2021, the total number of workers was about 1.6 million below where it would be otherwise. Legal immigration rates and refugee admissions have returned to prepandemic levels under the Biden administration, the analysts found, but are not enough to make up the difference.\par \par \uc2 Jason Karaian contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Jason Karaian contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY  FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220604ei640003p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
