\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Adams Briefs Employers on Public Safety\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emma Goldberg and Dana Rubinstein \par \uc2 653 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In his quest to get more workers back to the city, the mayor tried to reassure employers that he was taking steps to address crime, especially on the subways.\par \par \uc2 The New York City mayor, Eric Adams, held a briefing with more than 100 employers and business leaders on Thursday to discuss public safety in the city and the steps his administration is taking to ensure workers returning to the office feel comfortable during their commutes.\par \par \uc2 The Partnership for New York City, a business group, convened the call after the mayor heard concerns from employers about safety after Daniel Enriquez, 48, was fatally shot in the chest on the Q train last weekend. He was an employee of \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Concern about public safety, especially in the city's transit system, was the single largest issue workers cited as an obstacle to returning to the office, according to a poll conducted in March by Morning Consult on behalf of the Partnership for New York City. The death of Mr. Enriquez was the most recent of several high-profile violent incidents on the subway this year.\par \par \uc2 ''What employers were saying is, 'We're hearing from our employees that this is the obstacle in terms of returning to work -- returning to the subways,''' said \uc2 Kathryn Wylde\uc2 , head of the Partnership. ''The mayor was emphasizing that he is totally committed to the sense of a safe city, in terms of reducing crime and improving the perception of personal security in the city.''\par \par \uc2 Data released this month by the Partnership showed that return to office has been slower than employers expected: 38 percent of Manhattan office workers were on site as of mid-April, but more than a third of employers had projected that daily attendance would exceed 50 percent by then.\par \par \uc2 During the briefing this week with business leaders, the mayor shared his commitment to expanding the presence of police officers and mental-health workers throughout the city's transit system and promised a more comprehensive approach to supporting the city's homeless.\par \par \uc2 ''We wanted to show them what we are doing so they can go back to their employees and say we are responding to this issue of crime,'' Mr. Adams said Thursday, during a news conference announcing the city's bid to host the 2024 Democratic National Convention. Some of his critics say a bigger police presence alone won't be enough.\par \par \uc2 Despite research indicating that hybrid work is here to stay, Mr. Adams has made it his mission to get employees to return to the office.\par \par \uc2 He has argued that employees have a role to play in reviving the city's economy, including by patronizing small businesses in the central business districts. And he and his administration have made a full-court press behind the scenes.\par \par \uc2 In early March, Mr. Adams hosted several business leaders at a vegan dinner at Gracie Mansion, the mayor's residence. Jeff T. Blau, the chief executive of \uc2 Related Companies\uc2 , the real estate developer, attended, as did \uc2 James Gorman\uc2 , the chief executive of \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 ; \uc2 Jonathan Gray\uc2 , the president of \uc2 Blackstone\uc2 ; and Thasunda Brown Duckett, the chief executive of TIAA, according to \uc2 Charles Phillips\uc2 , the managing partner and co-founder of Recognize, a technology growth equity firm, who helped organize the dinner.\par \par \uc2 ''We want people back into the office all over the city,'' Mr. Phillips said. ''It's an issue if employees feel it's not safe, and the C.E.O.s need a script to be able to legitimately talk to the employees.''\par \par \uc2 Keechant Sewell\uc2 , the New York City police commissioner, has also been meeting with business leaders to convey a similar message, according to The New York Post.\par \par \uc2 Safety on the subway is one of New York office workers' biggest concerns. (PHOTOGRAPH BY DAKOTA SANTIAGO FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220528ei5s00045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
