\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Workers at \uc2 Activision\uc2  Vote to Form New Union\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kellen Browning \par \uc2 599 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The new union affects only a small group of workers -- 28 quality-assurance workers at \uc2 Activision\uc2 's Raven studio.\par \par \uc2 A group of workers at a video game studio that is part of \uc2 Activision Blizzard\uc2  has voted to form a union, a first for a major North American video game company.\par \par \uc2 The vote, which passed 19 to 3, affects 28 quality-assurance employees at Raven Software, the Wisconsin studio that helps to develop the popular Call of Duty game. The workers voted over the past several weeks, and the results were tallied by the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  on Monday. \uc2 Activision\uc2  has one week to formally object if it finds grounds for complaint.\par \par \uc2 The new union, the Game Workers Alliance, is the culmination of months of labor organizing at \uc2 Activision\uc2 , which has faced increasing pressure from employees to improve working conditions after a lawsuit accused the company of having a sexist culture in which women were routinely harassed.\par \par \uc2 Organizing at Raven in particular increased in intensity in December, when quality-assurance, or Q.A., workers walked out to protest the ending of about a dozen workers' contracts. The Communications Workers of America, a prominent tech, media and communications union, helped lead the unionization effort.\par \par \uc2 ''Our biggest hope is that our union serves as inspiration for the growing movement of workers organizing at video game studios to create better games and build workplaces that reflect our values and empower all of us,'' workers in the new union said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Sara Steffens\uc2 , the secretary-treasurer of the C.W.A., said that she was ''thrilled'' to welcome the new union and that ''these workers will soon have an enforceable union contract and a voice on the job.'' Senator \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2 , Democrat of Wisconsin, also cheered the new union on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Employees in the video game industry have complained for years about poor pay, gender discrimination and ''crunch'' -- a term for arduous, 12-to-14-hour shifts given to workers in a rush to meet deadlines. Those crunches particularly affect Q.A. workers, who say they are often treated as second-class workers. In recent years, employees have begun to organize. But until now, none of the major North American video game developers had a union.\par \par \uc2 The new union affects only a small group of workers -- the 28 Q.A. workers at the Raven studio, where several hundred people work. \uc2 Activision\uc2 , which is in the process of being acquired by \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  for $70 billion, had argued that all workers at the studio should be eligible to vote. The N.L.R.B. rejected that assertion at a hearing in April.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, \uc2 Activision\uc2  repeated its objection, arguing that the decision to unionize ''should not be made by 19 Raven employees.'' The company would not say whether it planned to file an objection, saying only that it was ''committed to doing what's best for the studio and our employees.''\par \par \uc2 An N.L.R.B. regional director has ''found merit to the allegations'' levied by C.W.A. that \uc2 Activision\uc2  violated federal labor law by telling employees not to talk about wages or workplace conditions, maintaining an ''overly broad'' social media policy and surveilling employees who were engaging in ''protected concerted activity.'' The labor board said it would issue a complaint against \uc2 Activision\uc2  if it could not settle the case.\par \par \uc2 Activision Blizzard is in the process of being acquired by Microsoft for $70 billion. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Adam Amengual for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220524ei5o0003j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
