\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  Says It Will Cover Travel Costs for Abortions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emma Goldberg \par \uc2 424 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In a memo on Monday, the company said it wanted to ensure that its employees had ''access to quality health care.''\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  announced on Monday that it would reimburse travel expenses for employees who need abortions and cannot get them within 100 miles of their homes, joining the small group of companies that have moved to protect employee abortion access as the Supreme Court appears likely to soon overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Sara Kelly, the company's acting vice president for partner resources, said she was ''deeply concerned'' reading the Supreme Court draft opinion, obtained this month by Politico, that would strike down the 1973 abortion ruling.\par \par \uc2 ''I know this is weighing on many of you, so let me be clear upfront -- regardless of what the Supreme Court ends up deciding, we will always ensure our partners have access to quality health care,'' Ms. Kelly wrote in a memo to \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  employees on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is among a handful of companies, including \uc2 Yelp\uc2 , \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  and \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , that have announced plans to cover transportation costs for employees in need of abortions. Most companies and business leaders, though, have stayed quiet since the Supreme Court's draft opinion was leaked.\par \par \uc2 According to findings from \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a research organization that supports abortion rights, the average distance to an abortion provider would increase by 97 miles, to 122 miles from 25 miles, if Roe is overturned.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has 240,000 employees and nearly 9,000 company-operated stores in the United States. The company declined to share how many employees it had in Texas and other states that will ban abortions quickly if the Supreme Court overturns Roe.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said its decision to cover travel costs for employees to get abortions added to its already expansive health care benefits, which include insurance coverage for gender-affirming care and costs associated with surrogacy, adoption and fertility treatments. The company said on Monday that it would also begin to cover travel expenses for gender-affirming care for employees who cannot get it near their homes, as lawmakers introduce anti-trans legislation across the country.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 's customers and workers tend to be more liberal, something that experts suggest has contributed to the growing union drive underway at more than 250 stores.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has not determined precisely when the travel reimbursements for abortions will take effect.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks wants to ensure workers' ''access to quality health care.'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY AUDRA MELTON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220517ei5h0004x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
