\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Exploring the Union Revival\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sabrina Tavernise \par \uc2 780 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The United States is seeing a revival in union membership; in the last six months the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  has recorded a 60 percent increase in workers filing for the petitions that allow union elections to take place. Noam Scheiber, a business reporter, said that in seven years covering unions for The Times, he has ''never seen a jump that big.'' The circumstances that have prompted these efforts bear some similarities to the 1930s, when a strike at Midwest plants led \uc2 General Motors\uc2  to recognize the \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  and sparked the largest gain in union membership in U.S. history.'' On a recent episode of ''The Daily,'' the host Sabrina Tavernise spoke with Mr. Scheiber about the comparisons. Read an edited and condensed excerpt.\par \par \uc2 Noam, what about the 1930s helps us understand what might happen today?\par \par \uc2 There are a number of similarities between today and the 1930s. Obviously, you have the trauma that workers felt during the pandemic, which is not so different than the trauma that workers felt during the Depression. Suddenly you feel vulnerable, you feel betrayed, you feel like these things that you knew to be solid have disappeared, and you want an institution -- maybe something like a union -- to give you more security going forward.\par \par \uc2 The second thing is you have a political environment, a regulatory environment, that's pretty friendly to unions. Joe Biden, like Franklin D. Roosevelt in the 1930s, has been outspoken in support of unions. Some of the federal agencies that work in this area have also been supportive of workers seeking to form unions.\par \par \uc2 Then you have to think about where we are in the economy today. Just like in the 1930s, we had this traumatic event. But since then, things have started to get better. We now have labor shortages. Employers are constantly talking about how they can't get enough workers in the door, but at the same time workers feel like they're not sharing in all the gains that the companies are making as the economy bounces back. So they feel a kind of disconnect, in the same way they did in the 1930s.\par \par \uc2 The last thing is, you have to look at the type of worker who's at the center of this unionization trend. If you think back to the 1930s, the people at G.M. were not the most marginal worker. We're seeing something similar today, where many of the people who are often leading this unionization campaign are college educated.\par \par \uc2 Why college grads?\par \par \uc2 It really goes back to the Great Recession of 2008. That was a deep and painful recession. People who had recently graduated from college had an incredibly difficult time finding jobs that were on a par with their skill set.\par \par \uc2 I remember interviewing college grads in Washington around 2010. They were still scooping ice cream.\par \par \uc2 In a typical recession, that might happen for a year or two after the recession. But the economics research that we've seen in recent years shows that these effects actually continued until about a decade or so after the Great Recession, really right up until the eve of the pandemic. There are some very prominent examples that I think all of us have come to recognize since then.\par \par \uc2 There are a number of people that I've interviewed who are involved in union campaigns now who fit this profile.You see a lot of these college-educated folks who, for one reason or another, often having to do with the economy and not having other opportunities, end up at companies like \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 . When they get there, they don't always love it.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic probably makes life even harder for the workers you were interviewing.That's right. In a way, I think of what happened after the Great Recession and up until the pandemic as kind of the kindling. Then you have this pandemic, which really is kind of the spark. .\par \par \uc2 Does this mean that unions are about to take off?\par \par \uc2 We don't know, but it's not necessarily a slam dunk. There are two big reasons to keep in mind here. Number one, workers just have less power today than they did in the 1930s. That's largely because of the trends in globalization and automation since then. The second reason is that companies may be more motivated today to crush unions than they were even back in the 1930s.\par \par \uc2 The explosion of unionization in the 1930s bears similarities to the current rise in union efforts. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ROBERT F. WAGNER LABOR ARCHIVES, UNION LABEL COLLECTION) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220505ei550005g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
