\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Companies; \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  Allows Employees To Continue Remote Work\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emma Goldberg \par \uc2 285 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 April 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The company, a proponent of more flexible workplace models, previously set a September 2022 date for a return to its offices.\par \par \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  told its 6,000 employees on Thursday that they would have the option to permanently work remotely, joining a growing number of companies that have ditched return-to-office plans, including Zillow and PwC. Airbnb also told its U.S. employees that they could move to anywhere in the country without a reduction to their compensation.\par \par \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  previously aimed to return employees to the office in September 2022. But unsurprisingly, its chief executive, \uc2 Brian Chesky\uc2 , has been a proponent of workplace models that give people more flexibility to travel, noting that about one in five guests last year reported using \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  to work remotely.\par \par \uc2 Airbnb\uc2 's remote-work policy is the latest in its efforts to encourage people to use \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  listings as pseudo-offices. About 10 percent of American workers are still remote, down from one-third in May 2020 at the height of pandemic lockdowns. Last summer, the company introduced Live Anywhere on \uc2 Airbnb\uc2 , a yearlong program that gave 12 people the chance to travel the globe while working from \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  locations.\par \par \uc2 Taking another tack, some tech companies, like \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , have tried in recent weeks to create office environments that are as alluring as vacation spots, offering games like corn hole, along with fried chicken and wine tastings for employees going back to their commutes.\par 