\par\par \uc2 Metropolitan Desk; SECT\par \b \uc2 Labor Union For Staff Of Buildings Reaches Deal\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patrick McGeehan \par \uc2 860 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 April 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 The New York Times on the Web\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The contract with Local 32BJ of the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2 , which represents workers at more than 3,000 apartment buildings in New York City, had been set to expire on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The union representing doormen and other workers in more than 3,000 apartment buildings in New York City reached a new contract agreement on Tuesday, averting a strike that could have left thousands of tenants standing guard in lobbies, sorting mail and hauling trash to the curb.\par \par \uc2 The agreement between Local 32BJ of the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2  and the Realty Advisory Board on Labor Relations was announced on Tuesday afternoon. It headed off what would have been the first strike by the city's unionized doormen in more than 30 years.\par \par \uc2 The Realty Advisory Board, which represents companies that employ more than 30,000 doormen, porters and building superintendents across the city, had been seeking concessions on health care premiums and other benefits, including sick pay and vacation time. The board argued that its employees were among just 5 percent of workers who do not contribute to their health insurance.\par \par \uc2 But Kyle Bragg, president of 32BJ, said those givebacks were deal-breakers and his members voted last week to authorize a strike if no contract deal was reached by Wednesday night, when the current contract expires. The union held a rally on Park Avenue last week that was attended by Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , Senator \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and other elected officials.\par \par \uc2 Despite the tough initial stance the board took, the union said that it secured satisfactory wage increases without giving up any benefits.\par \par \uc2 Annual wage increases averaging 3 percent over four years will bring the pay for a typical doorman (most of the doormen are male) to approximately $62,000 by the end of the contract in 2026, the two sides announced. The agreement also includes a $3,000 bonus this year, they said.\par \par \uc2 ''We have a deal!'' Mr. Bragg said in a statement. ''We got a deal done that protects health care, with no premium sharing. We got a deal done that protects paid time off. We got a deal done that provides the economic security our members need in a time of rising inflation.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bragg said the contract honored the ''indispensable'' contributions and sacrifices made by 32BJ's members during the pandemic. He said that by showing up for work every day during the pandemic they made it possible for tenants to work from home. Union officials said that 40 members of the doormen's union died from Covid-19 in the last two years.\par \par \uc2 ''They were there, keeping our buildings running and our communities safe, when the city needed them most,'' Mr. Bragg said.\par \par \uc2 The contract still must be ratified by a vote of the union's members and the board's directors.\par \par \uc2 Howard Rothschild, president of the Realty Advisory Board, said the agreement was fair and would continue to create and support middle class jobs for more than 30,000 workers.\par \par \uc2 ''There is great respect, admiration and gratitude for the employees,'' Mr. Rothschild said. ''They did respond appropriately. They did come to work every day. And they should be rewarded for that.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Rothschild said the increases in the contract were the biggest the board had ever granted, ''much, much higher'' than in any past contract. ''They asked that their employees be acknowledged and rewarded and we were more than happy to do that,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 For many New Yorkers, living in a ''doorman building'' has not only been a luxury but a necessity. Doormen and their colleagues provide a measure of security and handle chores like package delivery for their tenants, who live in about 550,000 apartments in the city.\par \par \uc2 Early in the pandemic, while many residents were holed up in their apartments, the building service workers took on greater burdens, Mr. Bragg said.\par \par \uc2 ''You think about all the deliveries that have doubled, tripled, quadrupled,'' Mr. Bragg said. ''People were not going out. They had grocery deliveries, food deliveries, dry cleaning, laundry, a lot of things that people would normally be out and about taking care of personally. Our members had to pick up that increase in load.''\par \par \uc2 As contract negotiations dragged on this year, some buildings were preparing to make other arrangements to keep their buildings safe and clean. One luxury condominium in Midtown had sent a notice to its tenants this month seeking volunteers to work alongside security guards who would have been hired in the event of a strike.\par \par \uc2 Doormen typically know a building's residents and can permit or deny access accordingly. But newly hired guards, ''who are not familiar with your building and do not know the residents,'' the notice said, ''will have difficulty effectively performing this task during the busy afternoon and evening rush hours.''\par \par \uc2 ''Please sign up at the doorman's station for this important and necessary responsibility,'' the notice continued. ''We are asking for volunteers to assist with trash removal and front desk security.''\par \par \uc2 A contract agreement with the union was announced on Tuesday afternoon. CINDY ORD/GETTY IMAGES \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220420ei4k0006r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
