\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Judge Rules \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Must Reinstate Worker It Fired\b0\par\par\uc2 By Karen Weise \par \uc2 962 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 April 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The judge said the worker was ''unlawfully'' fired two years ago after a pandemic safety protest at a company warehouse.\par \par \uc2 A judge ruled on Monday that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  must reinstate and pay lost wages to a worker the company ''unlawfully'' fired two years ago after a protest at its fulfillment center on Staten Island, the same warehouse that recently voted in a landmark election to unionize.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  regional director argued that the firing was retaliation for protesting safety conditions, which is protected by federal labor law. Benjamin W. Green, an administrative law judge, agreed.\par \par \uc2 The case centers on a verbal altercation during the early days of the pandemic in New York. On April 6, 2020, Gerald Bryson was protesting outside the warehouse, known as JFK8, and said it should be shut down for safety. Another employee said she wanted the facility to remain open because she was grateful for the extra pay she was receiving for working during the pandemic. The two exchanged insults, but only Mr. Bryson was fired. The woman received a written warning.\par \par \uc2 ''For me to win and walk back through those doors changes everything,'' Mr. Bryson said in an interview Monday. ''It will show that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  can be beat. It will show you have to fight for what you believe in.''\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has said Mr. Bryson was fired for violating its policy against vulgar and harassing language. It has defended its actions by pointing to an internal investigation it conducted and arguing that the punishment was consistent with how other workers were treated.\par \par \uc2 ''We strongly disagree with this ruling and are surprised the N.L.R.B. would want any employer to condone Mr. Bryson's behavior,'' Kelly Nantel, a company spokeswoman, said in a statement. ''Mr. Bryson was fired for bullying, cursing at and defaming a female co-worker over a bullhorn in front of the workplace.''\par \par \uc2 She added, ''We do not tolerate that type of conduct in our workplace and intend to file an appeal with the N.L.R.B.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Green, the judge, knocked \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's key justifications for the dismissal. He said \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's investigation had been ''skewed'' and designed to find reasons to fire Mr. Bryson for his protest. Noting that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  did not interview a protester who had recorded the argument, the judge wrote that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  ''preferred not to obtain information from someone who was protesting with Bryson even though that person was likely in the best position to explain what happened.''\par \par \uc2 He also doubted the statements of the managers and other employees whom \uc2 Amazon\uc2  did interview. \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , for example, documented that the woman, who is white, and a manager had said Mr. Bryson called her a racial slur during the altercation. But a video of the encounter showed that never happened. The woman told Mr. Bryson, who is Black, to ''go back to the Bronx,'' which the judge said Mr. Bryson could reasonably construe as being ''racial.''\par \par \uc2 ''I find it implausible that six individuals would view the argument and coincidentally provide these one-sided, exaggerated accounts unless such accounts were solicited from them,'' he wrote.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bryson, who celebrated the ruling with his 9-year-old son, said he was glad the judge had found that some of \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's public statements about him were not valid. ''I really feel they damaged my name over two years for nothing,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  justified the firing by saying other employees at the facility had been fired for similar behavior, but the judge disagreed. He said \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's records indicated lesser punishment ''for conduct more threatening than that of Bryson or which involved physical touching.'' None of the examples involved incidents outside the facility on unpaid time, he added.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Green also found that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  did not produce all of the documents asked for in a subpoena. He said \uc2 Amazon\uc2  must post notices inside the warehouse affirming the right for workers to form a union and publicly acknowledging the remedies it must take.\par \par \uc2 ''This is a very stern rebuke of \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's unlawful, retaliatory termination of Gerald,'' said Frank Kearl, a lawyer with \uc2 Make the Road New York\uc2 , a progressive advocacy group that represented Mr. Bryson.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  had fought the case at the labor agency and in federal court. To examine a witness in hearings last year, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  hired Zainab Ahmad, a lawyer at \uc2 Gibson, Dunn & Crutcher\uc2  and a former federal prosecutor who had tried cases against terrorists.\par \par \uc2 In a related proceeding, the labor board sued \uc2 Amazon\uc2  in federal court last month, asking a judge to order the company to reinstate Mr. Bryson because otherwise its ''serious flouting'' of the protections would ''continue unchecked.'' That case is still in progress.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  argued that the labor agency showed bias when it asked the federal judge to intervene just before the union election at JFK8. The company has cited Mr. Bryson's case as a key reason the union's victory should be thrown out.\par \par \uc2 The Amazon Labor Union, which won the vote at JFK8, is facing a second vote at a neighboring warehouse at the end of April.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bryson, who is active in the union, said the ruling bolstered the case it was making to workers. ''I am there to say, 'Listen, I just battled with them for two years and won,''' he said.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's appeal of Monday's ruling would go to the five-member board of the agency. If it loses there, it can challenge the result in federal court.\par \par \uc2 Gerald Bryson, a former worker at Amazon's JFK8 warehouse on Staten Island, argued he was fired in retaliation for protesting safety conditions. (PHOTOGRAPH BY DAVE SANDERS FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220419ei4j0003z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
