\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Remote Work Declines in U.S., but Some Resist Return to the Office\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emma Goldberg \par \uc2 957 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 April 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 At the height of the pandemic lockdowns in May 2020, more than one-third of U.S. workers were doing their jobs at least partly from home, shifting perceptions of workplace flexibility. Ever since, the share of workers telecommuting because of Covid-19 has steadily declined, falling to 22.7 percent of the work force in February 2021 and 10 percent last month, according to data from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now those numbers appear likely to dip lower. Many employers have heightened their calls for workers to come to the office in recent weeks, with companies including \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and \uc2 Chevron\uc2  asking some employees back, even as Covid case counts begin to climb again in some areas and mask mandates drop.\par \par \uc2 ''You see now lots of companies, big and small, starting to put stakes in the ground saying we want our people back in the office,'' said \uc2 Mark Ein\uc2 , the chair of \uc2 Kastle Systems\uc2 , a security firm whose tracker placed office occupancy at 40 percent last month.\par \par \uc2 But return-to-office plans have been met with skepticism from people who say working from home improves productivity and mental health. Some workers of color said telecommuting enabled them to avoid insensitive comments they faced in the office.\par \par \uc2 ''When I'm in a room that's not as diverse, they automatically think of me as the secretary,'' said Eleanore Fernandez, 50, an arts employee in the San Francisco Bay Area who switched to working remotely during the pandemic. ''They would ask me: 'Where can I get a good sandwich? Do you know where the kitchen is?'''\par \par \uc2 Some employers are reopening their workplaces but making commitments to long-term flexibility. \uc2 AppDirect\uc2 , a software company with 850 employees worldwide, called its return-to-office plan Janus -- for the Roman god of transitions and beginnings -- to signal that the next phase of on-site work won't look like prepandemic times. Employees are not required to go back if they prefer to stay home.\par \par \uc2 ''We're not saying that in office is better or working at home is better -- we're leaving it to be a choice,'' said Deb Tenenbaum, \uc2 AppDirect\uc2 's head of human resources.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Tenenbaum joined the company just before the pandemic and said she was excited by the prospect of building relationships and learning more about colleagues through in-person meetings: ''Maybe how tall they are,'' she said jokingly.\par \par \uc2 When pandemic lockdowns first swept the United States, the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  started collecting information about a new question for American workers: Who was working from home because of Covid-19?\par \par \uc2 Who are the remote workers?\par \par \uc2 Just under 19 percent of people with at least a bachelor's degree worked remotely at some point last month because of Covid, compared with 3 percent of high school graduates, and just over 1 percent of those with less than a high school diploma.\par \par \uc2 In May 2020, nearly 60 percent of people with a bachelor's degree or higher worked remotely at some point, compared with 15 percent of high school graduates and 5 percent of those with less than a high school diploma.\par \par \uc2 The share of women working remotely last month was slightly higher than that of men -- 10.7 percent compared with 9.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 About 9 percent of white workers, 8 percent of Black workers, 6 percent of Hispanic workers and 21 percent of Asian workers were working remotely at some point last month because of Covid. Those figures were 35 percent, 29 percent, 23 percent and 52 percent, respectively, in May 2020.\par \par \uc2 How does remote work break down by industry?\par \par \uc2 People in management and professional occupations have been more likely to work from home than those in service, construction, transportation and other manual jobs.\par \par \uc2 Last month, 18 percent of people in management and professional occupations worked from home at some point because of Covid. About a quarter of legal workers did. Fewer than 1 percent of those in food preparation, building and grounds cleaning, and installation and maintenance occupations worked from home. In leisure and hospitality, 3 percent of workers worked remotely.\par \par \uc2 In May 2020, about 57 percent of people in management and professional occupations worked from home at some point because of Covid. About three-quarters of legal workers did. In service occupations, the share of remote workers was lower: 4.5 percent of people in food preparation and serving, 4 percent of building and grounds cleaning, 2.5 percent of farming, fishing and forestry and 5 percent of transportation workers. In leisure and hospitality, 15 percent worked remotely.\par \par \uc2 How rapidly has the share of remote workers declined?\par \par \uc2 The share of Americans working remotely has declined pretty steadily, though it has sometimes increased when the virus surges: In November, 11 percent of people worked from home at some point. In January, amid the spread of the Omicron variant, that figure climbed to 15 percent.\par \par \uc2 How does the share of remote workers now compare to pre-Covid times?\par \par \uc2 This data wasn't collected prepandemic, so it's hard to know how much of a shift it represents from the before times. But Gallup polling indicates that in 2019 about 4 percent of employed people exclusively worked from home. Gallup polling also indicates that 32 percent of Americans in jobs that can be done remotely prefer to work exclusively remotely, and that 59 percent prefer a combination of remote and on-site work.\par \par \uc2 A telemarketing center in Alabama in April 2020. The share of workers telecommuting because of Covid dropped to 10 percent last month. (PHOTOGRAPH BY BOB MILLER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220402ei420004w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
