\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast Workers Announce They Have Formed a Companywide Union\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Robertson \par \uc2 485 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 March 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The union will cover more than 500 employees from all of Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast's brands, except the four publications that have already unionized.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of workers at the publishing giant Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast, which owns titles like Vogue, Vanity Fair, Bon App\u233\'00\'E9tit and GQ, announced on Tuesday that they had formed a companywide union. The Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast Union is affiliated with the NewsGuild of New York, which also represents editorial employees at \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  as well as other publications.\par \par \uc2 The union will cover more than 500 employees from all of Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast's brands, except for those from Ars Technica, Pitchfork, Wired and The New Yorker, which unionized separately with the NewsGuild in recent years. In a statement shared through the NewsGuild on Tuesday, the union said it had asked Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast management for voluntary recognition.\par \par \uc2 ''We plan to have productive and thoughtful conversations with them over the coming weeks to learn more,'' a Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast spokesman said. The company has voluntarily recognized the four existing unions.\par \par \uc2 The employees in the newly formed Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast union, including editorial, video and production staff, said in a statement that they were pushing for better pay, increased job security, and a stronger commitment to diversity and equity.\par \par \uc2 Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast has faced waves of internal turmoil in the past two years over the treatment of employees of color and the low wages of some workers. The Times first reported on the companywide organizing effort in December.\par \par \uc2 In 2021, tensions over contract negotiation talks for The New Yorker Union led to a vote by employees to authorize a strike and a protest in front of the Greenwich Village townhouse of \uc2 Anna Wintour\uc2 , Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast's global chief content officer and the editor of Vogue. The union reached a deal in June.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, Ms. Wintour and Roger Lynch, the chief executive, apologized to staff members for racial inequities at the company after a cultural reckoning in the wake of the murder of \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 , a Black man who was killed by a police officer in Minneapolis. Employees complained about racial inequality inside the company, and a top editor at Bon App\u233\'00\'E9tit resigned after the emergence of an old photo of him wearing a racially insensitive costume.\par \par \uc2 ''There is no viable 'future' of Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast if women and people of color continue to be used to fill a diversity quota,'' Cortni Spearman, a senior social media manager at Glamour and a member of the new Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast Union, said in a statement on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast, which owns titles like Vogue and Bon App\u233\'00\'E9tit, has faced waves of internal turmoil in the past two years over the treatment of employees of color and the low wages of some workers. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Jens Mortensen for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220330ei3u0003q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
